Q: Complex declarations In C A: <p>This declaration</p>
<pre><code> int ( *a )[5];
</code></pre>
<p>declares a pointer to an array of <strong>5</strong> elements.</p>
<p>You may rewrite the declaration using <strong>typedef</strong> like</p>
<pre><code>typedef int T[5];
T *a;
</code></pre>
<p>That is <strong>a</strong> is a pointer to objects of the array type <strong>int[5]</strong>.</p>
<p>You can further initialize the pointer by the address of an array as whole. For example</p>
<pre><code>int ( *a )[5] = NULL;
int array[5] = { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 };
a = &amp;array;
</code></pre>
<p>Or</p>
<pre><code>typedef int T[5];
T *a = NULL;
T array = { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 };
a = &amp;array;
</code></pre>
<p>Now dereferncing the pointer you get the array object pointed to by the pointer and you can for example output the array size and its elements the following way</p>
<pre><code>printf( &quot;The array size is %zu and it contains %zu elements\n&quot;,
        sizeof( *a ), sizeof( *a ) / sizeof( ( *a )[0] ) );
printf( &quot;The array contains the following elements:&quot; ); 
for ( int i = 0; i &lt; 5; i++ )
{
    printf( &quot; %d&quot;, ( *a )[i] );
}
putchar( '\n' );
</code></pre>

Q: When is it appropriate to use a semicolon? A: <h2>TL;DR: Always</h2>
<p>Keep in mind: <em>better safe than sorry</em></p>
<p>You should probably place them all the time. You don't <em>need</em> to place them in order for TypeScript to work, but you will avoid errors by doing so. <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/JavaScript_syntax#Whitespace_and_semicolons" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Automatic semicolon insertion</a> (ASI) works quite well most of time, but not always. Do you really want to run into a problem just because you didn't put a semicolon, and you keep overlooking the mistake? (Depending on your IDE, the mistake might actually be caught). But consider this perfectly valid JavaScript code.</p>
<pre><code> var x = { xx : &quot;hello&quot;, yy : &quot;world&quot;}
 (function () {
     console.log(&quot;Hello, World!&quot;);
 })();
</code></pre>
<p>This is valid JavaScript code (<strong>and thus valid TypeScript code</strong>). This code will actually give an error. <code>Uncaught TypeError: (intermediate value)(intermediate value) is not a function(…)</code>.</p>
<p>Which could be avoided by just placing a semicolon after the first line. You don't need it there, and if the next line wasn't that function line, it would probably work correctly. But you want to take that risk? It seems like taking the risk for mistakes over one extra character is not worth it to me. Plus, after a while, you just get used to place semicolons at the end of a line anyway;</p>
<h2>Think of your colleagues</h2>
<p>Another reason you might want to use them all the time is in the case of code changing. Your colleague might have to change your code - and in doing so thinks that the ASI will keep working even with his code change. Imagine that this is not the case, and that his change actually makes ASI do something wrong. Is it really worth that headache for your colleague? If he/she changes enough of your code and then suddenly runs into a lot of errors, he/she might be rather confused if he/she does not know the exact way in which ASI is working. You could save a potential colleague quite a bit of (unneccessary) work by just putting them everywhere.</p>
